      "page_title": "Why Streams Exist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What A Stream Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Abstraction for data arriving or consumed over time",
            "Delivered in chunks, never all at once",
            "Producer, bounded buffer, consumer — plus a feedback wire",
            "Carry that mental model through every chapter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "producer",
              "bounded buffer (hWM)",
              "consumer",
              "feedback back to producer"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The 10 GB Problem",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const b = fs.readFileSync('10gb.log')",
            "// 10 GB into RSS, then you start work",
            "fs.createReadStream('10gb.log')",
            "  .pipe(res)  // ~64 KB live at a time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Approach",
            "Memory",
            "First byte"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "readFileSync",
              "O(file size)",
              "after full read"
            ],
            [
              "createReadStream",
              "O(highWaterMark)",
              "after chunk 1"
            ],
            [
              "10 GB log",
              "10 GB vs 64 KB",
              "O(n) vs O(1)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Streamed memory is independent of input size",
            "Time-to-first-byte is constant, not proportional",
            "HTTP proxy writes to client before origin finishes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Where They Already Live",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Core API",
            "Stream type"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "fs.createReadStream",
              "Readable"
            ],
            [
              "http.IncomingMessage",
              "Readable"
            ],
            [
              "http.ServerResponse",
              "Writable"
            ],
            [
              "zlib.createGzip",
              "Transform"
            ],
            [
              "net.Socket",
              "Duplex"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "crypto.createHash — writable sink, transform variants exist",
            "process.stdin readable, process.stdout writable",
            "You have used streams already without naming them"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const ac = new AbortController()",
            "pipeline(a, b, c, { signal: ac.signal })",
            "ac.abort()   // rejects with AbortError",
            "finished(res, err => cleanup(err)) // 1 stream"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two Free Properties",
          "items": [
            "read | transform | transform | write composes",
            "Small single-purpose parts, chained",
            "Streams are EventEmitters: on/emit/once",
            "So the API is event-driven, not promise-first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Read side and write side need no relationship",
            "Transform is a Duplex whose read side derives from writes",
            "crypto.createCipheriv is the other classic Transform"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Chunks, Pipes & The Four Types",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Carry this",
          "text": "Bounded buffer plus a backwards signal. Every later chapter is a detail of those two things."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. What A Chunk Holds"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Buffer by default — raw bytes",
            "String when an encoding is set",
            "Any JS value in object mode"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Unix Pipes In JS"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "fs.createReadStream('in.txt')",
            "  .pipe(zlib.createGzip())   // transform",
            "  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('out.gz'))",
            "// constant memory, any input size"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Four At A Glance",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Role",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Readable",
              "source you read",
              "fs.createReadStream"
            ],
            [
              "Writable",
              "sink you write",
              "fs.createWriteStream"
            ],
            [
              "Duplex",
              "two channels",
              "net.Socket"
            ],
            [
              "Transform",
              "output from input",
              "zlib.createGzip"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Readable emits data, readable, end",
            "Writable has write() and end(), emits drain, finish",
            "Server request is readable; server response is writable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Duplex Is Two Wires",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const s = net.connect(80, 'example.com')",
            "s.write('GET / HTTP/1.0\\r\\n\\r\\n') // to peer",
            "s.on('data', d => log(d))      // from peer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. PassThrough",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A Transform that outputs its input unchanged",
            "Use as a tee point to observe bytes flowing by",
            "Use as a placeholder stage, or a small buffer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Class Hierarchy"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
