      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Stream Hooks & Flowing vs Paused",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Which Side You Implement"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "You write"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Readable",
              "_read(size)"
            ],
            [
              "Writable",
              "_write(chunk, enc, cb)"
            ],
            [
              "Transform",
              "_transform + _flush"
            ],
            [
              "Duplex",
              "_read and _write"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Underscore = internal contract",
          "text": "You implement _read/_write/_transform. You never call them. The public read/write/push call them for you."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Duplex Or Transform?"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Output derived from input — Transform",
            "Two unrelated channels in one object — Duplex",
            "Every type takes { objectMode: true } in its options"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Paused Is The Default",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every readable starts paused, buffering nothing out",
            "Flowing starts when you attach a data handler",
            "Or call .resume(), or .pipe() somewhere"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Flowing Mode",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "rs.on('data', c => use(c))  // now flowing",
            "rs.pause()                  // back to paused",
            "rs.resume()                 // flowing again"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Chunks are pushed at you as fast as the source runs",
            "Removing all data listeners does NOT re-pause",
            "The mode stays flowing until you pause explicitly"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Danger",
          "text": "A data listener with no destination throws backpressure away — you are draining the source into memory, or into nothing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Paused Mode Pulls",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "rs.on('readable', () => {",
            "  let c",
            "  while ((c = rs.read()) !== null)",
            "    use(c)   // you pull, at your pace",
            "})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "read(n) returns n bytes, or null if fewer buffered",
            "read() with no argument returns everything buffered",
            "null means wait, not end-of-stream"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Lifecycle Order"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "readable",
              "data",
              "end (source exhausted)",
              "close (resources released)"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Consuming & Building a Readable",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "end never fires if you never consume the stream",
            "close means the fd or handle is released"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Encoding, Not toString"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "rs.setEncoding('utf8')  // chunks are strings",
            "// multi-byte chars split across chunks",
            "// are rejoined; chunk.toString() is not"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. The Modern Default"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "for await (const chunk of readable) {",
            "  await save(chunk)  // backpressure free",
            "}   // errors arrive as exceptions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Helpers Worth Knowing",
          "items": [
            "Readable.from(iterable) — build from any (async) iterable",
            "stream.toArray() — collect on modern Node",
            "stream.toWeb() — hand off to Web Streams"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Contract",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Implement _read(size); call this.push(chunk) inside it",
            "Push once or many times per _read call",
            "this.push(null) signals EOF",
            "No push(null) means the consumer's end never fires"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Minimal Counter",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "let i = 0",
            "const rs = new Readable({ read() {",
            "  this.push(i < 5 ? String(i++) : null)",
            "}})   // null ends it after five chunks"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. push() Returns A Signal",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "false means the buffer passed highWaterMark",
            "A hint: stop pushing until _read is called again",
            "_read is never re-entrant — Node calls it after a push",
            "Or after read() drains the internal buffer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "Pushing in a tight loop without checking push()'s return defeats highWaterMark — the buffer grows without bound."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Async Readables & the write() Protocol",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Async Sources"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Readable({ read() {",
            "  fetchNext()",
            "    .then(v => this.push(v))",
            "    .catch(e => this.destroy(e)) // no throw",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never throw synchronously out of _read",
            "Report failure with this.destroy(err)",
            "destroy emits error then close for you",
            "Never emit('error') by hand"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Object Mode"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Readable({ objectMode: true, read() {",
            "  this.push({ id: 1 })  // any JS value",
            "}})  // highWaterMark counts objects, def 16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Usually Skip All This"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "async function* rows() { yield await next() }",
            "const rs = Readable.from(rows())",
            "// replaces most hand-written _read code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "When to hand-roll",
          "text": "Prefer Readable.from unless you need fine control over pacing, pooling, or the exact push timing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. write() Answers You",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Return",
            "Meaning",
            "Do"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "true",
              "buffer below hWM",
              "keep writing"
            ],
            [
              "false",
              "at or over hWM",
              "stop, wait for drain"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Advisory, not enforced",
          "text": "Writes after false are still accepted and still buffered. That is exactly how process memory blows up under load."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Correct Protocol",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "function push() {",
            "  while (i < n && stream.write(data[i++]));",
            "  if (i < n) stream.once('drain', push)",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Stop the moment write() returns false",
            "Resume only when drain fires",
            "once, not on — otherwise handlers pile up"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Ending And Its Events",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Event",
            "Fires when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "finish",
              "end() called, buffer flushed"
            ],
            [
              "close",
              "underlying resource released"
            ],
            [
              "error",
              "then close, on failure"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Writable Internals & Why Backpressure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "end([chunk]) writes a last chunk and finishes",
            "write() after end throws ERR_STREAM_WRITE_AFTER_END",
            "finish does not imply the fd is closed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Batching With cork"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "res.cork()",
            "res.write('a'); res.write('b')",
            "process.nextTick(() => res.uncork())"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fewer, larger syscalls to the underlying resource",
            "Always pair cork with uncork",
            "Uncork on the next tick, not later"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Implementing One"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Writable({ write(chunk, enc, cb) {",
            "  db.insert(chunk, cb)  // cb exactly once",
            "}})   // cb(err) on failure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The Other Two Hooks",
          "items": [
            "_writev(chunks, cb) — batched, used when corked or queued",
            "_final(cb) — runs once before finish",
            "_final is where you flush trailers or close handles",
            "Calling the write callback twice throws"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Hot-path gotcha",
          "text": "A synchronous callback() from _write can starve the event loop. process.nextTick(callback) is the usual fix."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Stated Once",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Feedback from a slow consumer to a fast producer",
            "Meaning: slow down, I am not keeping up",
            "Without it, the difference lands in your heap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Producer",
            "Consumer",
            "Grows at"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "100 MB/s",
              "10 MB/s",
              "90 MB/s"
            ],
            [
              "10 s later",
              "—",
              "900 MB in RSS"
            ],
            [
              "1 min later",
              "—",
              "OOM kill"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Bounded Buffer",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "highWaterMark"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "byte streams",
              "64 KB (65536)"
            ],
            [
              "object mode",
              "16 items"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Threshold, not a cap",
          "text": "Node keeps buffering past highWaterMark. Crossing it only flips the signal from true to false."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Backpressure Signals & Tuning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Two Signals",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Side",
            "Signal",
            "Cleared by"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "writable",
              "write() false",
              "drain event"
            ],
            [
              "readable",
              "push() false",
              "next _read()"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. What pipe Does"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// pipe, in two lines",
            "if (!dest.write(chunk)) src.pause()",
            "dest.on('drain', () => src.resume())"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "source pauses",
              "transform stalls",
              "sink says false",
              "signal travels backwards"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "In a 3-stage pipeline it propagates stage by stage",
            "The sink stalls the transform, which stalls the source",
            "One slow consumer throttles the whole chain"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Tuning The Mark"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "hWM",
            "Gain",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "bigger",
              "throughput",
              "memory"
            ],
            [
              "smaller",
              "latency, memory",
              "more switches"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Diagnosing A Leak"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Watch writableLength and readableLength over time",
            "Rising steadily = one side ignores the signal",
            "Pair it with RSS growth to confirm"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "Not using pipe, pipeline, or for await? Then backpressure is your job by hand — and probably wrong."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Shape",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "write() in",
              "_transform",
              "push() out",
              "readable side"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sits mid-pipeline: written chunks in, pushed chunks out",
            "Implement _transform(chunk, encoding, callback)",
            "Push zero, one, or many chunks, then call the callback"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Transform Patterns & _flush",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. One In, One Out",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({ transform(c, e, cb) {",
            "  this.push(c.toString().toUpperCase())",
            "  cb()   // 1:1, simplest case",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. One In, Many Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({ transform(c, e, cb) {",
            "  const p = (this.tail + c).split('\\n')",
            "  this.tail = p.pop()   // keep remainder",
            "  p.forEach(l => this.push(l)); cb()",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Many In, One Out",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({ objectMode: true,",
            " transform(o, e, cb) { this.rows.push(o)",
            "  if (this.rows.length < 100) return cb()",
            "  this.push(this.rows); this.rows = []; cb()",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Tail You Forget"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({ transform: t, flush(cb) {",
            "  if (this.tail) this.push(this.tail)",
            "  cb()  // no flush = last line vanishes",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "_flush runs after the last chunk, before end",
            "Only place to emit a trailing line or final batch",
            "Omitting it drops the tail silently — no error"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Failing And Awaiting"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({ async transform(c, e, cb) {",
            "  try { cb(null, await work(c)) }",
            "  catch (err) { cb(err) } // not auto-caught",
            "}})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "callback(err) reports failure and stops the pipeline",
            "callback(null, chunk) is shorthand for one push",
            "A rejected promise inside _transform is not caught for you"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The #1 transform bug",
          "text": "Chunk boundaries are arbitrary. One input chunk is never one logical record — buffer the remainder."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Know These Three",
          "items": [
            "zlib.createGzip / createGunzip",
            "crypto.createCipheriv",
            "stream.PassThrough",
            "objectMode both sides = the ETL workhorse"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What pipe Gives You",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Wires data and backpressure between two streams",
            "Returns dest, so a.pipe(b).pipe(c) chains",
            "That is the entire feature list"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The workaround is the point",
          "text": "on('error') per stage plus manual destroy() everywhere is exactly what pipeline already does for you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "pipe() Pitfalls vs pipeline()",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. What pipe Skips",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Errors are NOT forwarded in either direction",
            "An error on a leaves b and c open, un-destroyed",
            "No auto-destroy: sockets hang until timeout",
            "Each stage needs its own listener if you insist"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. pipeline, Promise Form",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const { pipeline } =",
            "  require('node:stream/promises')",
            "await pipeline(rs, gzip, ws)  // rejects",
            "// destroys every stream on fail or finish"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Callback form: pipeline(a, b, c, cb)",
            "One handler catches an error from any stage",
            "The error object tells you which stage failed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Generators And Aborts"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "await pipeline(src, async function* (s) {",
            "  for await (const c of s) yield up(c)",
            "}, dest)   // async gen is a legal stage"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Side By Side"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "pipe",
            "pipeline"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "errors",
              "manual per stage",
              "centralized"
            ],
            [
              "cleanup",
              "none",
              "auto destroy"
            ],
            [
              "returns",
              "dest",
              "cb or promise"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Stream Errors & Object Mode",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule",
          "text": "pipeline in all application code. Raw pipe only in a one-liner script where a crash is acceptable."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Failing A Stream"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unhandled error event throws and kills the process",
            "destroy(err) emits error, then close, stops I/O",
            "After it, .destroyed is true and writes are rejected",
            "autoDestroy defaults true — streams self-destroy after end"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Codes To Recognise"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE",
              "stage closed early"
            ],
            [
              "ERR_STREAM_WRITE_AFTER_END",
              "wrote past end()"
            ],
            [
              "ERR_STREAM_DESTROYED",
              "used after destroy"
            ],
            [
              "EPIPE",
              "peer went away"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Leak & Loop Traps",
          "items": [
            "An error must destroy the other stages, or fds leak",
            "EMFILE: too many open files traces back to leaked pipes",
            "for await turns errors into try/catch exceptions",
            "break destroys the stream — usually what you want"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "HTTP trap",
          "text": "Once the response body is streaming, headers are already sent — you cannot switch to a 500, only destroy the socket."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What The Flag Changes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Chunks may be any JS value except null",
            "null still means EOF, never a value",
            "highWaterMark counts items, default 16",
            "Huge behavioral gap from the 64 KB byte default"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "Pushing a legitimate null in object mode ends the stream. Wrap the value, or push a sentinel object."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Sides Set Separately",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "new Transform({",
            "  writableObjectMode: false, // Buffers in",
            "  readableObjectMode: true   // objects out",
            "})  // the parser shape"
          ]
        }
      ]
